WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.590
I think jewelry is the most
magical thing you can wear

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.010
For something so small that can fit

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:13.700
in the palm of your hand,

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.943
it can hold a world of
memories and emotions.

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:23.500
It has the ability to reach into the past

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.810
as well as reach into the future,

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:28.653
and I find that endearingly romantic.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.313
A lot of jewelry embodies
a lot of human suffering,

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:40.240
whether that is the suffering of miners,

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.070
whether it's the environmental disruption,

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:46.990
they produce something very beautiful,

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:50.875
but it often comes at
a kind of nasty cost.

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:55.710
The more people get concerned about that,

00:00:55.710 --> 00:01:00.133
the more they are open to alternatives.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.450
In this place, we mine the
sky to get the gases we need

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:08.663
to make diamonds.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:13.580
Our entire ingredient
list is the wind, the sun,

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:16.423
the rain and atmospheric
carbon, that's it, no more.

00:01:18.498 --> 00:01:20.760
There is still so much to explore

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.060
in the world of science and
also material innovation

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:27.920
and how to turn something
negative and wasteful

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.023
into the things that we want.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:37.680
Anabela Chan is telling a new
21st century romantic story

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:42.700
about jewelry, which is
that the designer's eye

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:47.293
is just as important as the
materials they're made from.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:52.710
I've made pieces for everyone
from sort of Beyonce,

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:57.710
to Rihanna, to Taylor Swift,
Julia Roberts, Lupita Nyong'o.

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:01.273
From the Oscars to the Grammys.

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:07.210
When I told people I wanted to create fine

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:10.380
and high jewelry using
laboratory grown gemstones,

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.157
people thought I was crazy .

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:25.970
There are several pieces
from my own collection

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:27.680
that I treasure.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.090
One of them is this ring
from the late Andrew Grima.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:35.130
This was one of his sort of
architectural inspired rings

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:37.320
that he designed in the '70s.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:41.990
He is known as the father of
contemporary jewelry design.

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:45.740
He famously said that a 50 carat Topaz

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:49.410
can become a work of art
that can be worn and enjoyed,

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:52.560
yet a 60 sort of carat
diamond needs to be locked up

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.630
in a vault.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:03:00.630
Traditionally, high fine jewelry
is notoriously exclusive.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:03.630
I wanted it to be more accessible,

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:06.900
because it is the ultimate craftsmanship.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:10.730
It's the ultimate sort
of handcrafted treasure

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:12.650
that shouldn't just be accessible

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:14.653
by so few people in the world.

00:03:20.123 --> 00:03:24.070
The most beautiful colors
and geometries and textures

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:25.750
come from nature.

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:27.990
And a lot of the times when I design

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:31.370
I'm merely trying to
capture that moment of magic

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:34.890
I see in nature, transforming
that into something

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:36.083
that can be worn.

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.200
I would always start
by having a sketchbook

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.670
and my drawing easel,

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:44.853
and going to draw
subjects that interest me.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:50.803
Just seeing something ordinary
as something extraordinary.

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:56.240
The two pieces we're making
are a pair of broaches

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:00.210
inspired by a blade of
grass with morning dew drops

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:02.223
sort of running through the veins.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:11.163
I will then start to sketch
each piece of jewelry in pencil.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:15.390
And then from those initial sketches,

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:16.993
there could be hundreds of them,

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:20.500
I will then take sort of my favorite ones,

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.880
and then transition them into paints

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.940
which is known as jewelry renderings.

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:30.940
That will essentially be
like a map for the craftsmen

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:32.833
to work with.

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:39.080
I was trained very
traditionally at art college

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:41.140
as a fine jewelry designer,

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:43.650
working with sort of mined gemstones,

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:47.400
and I think what really sort
of changed my trajectory

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.493
was when I went on my
honeymoon to Sri Lanka.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:55.210
I knew that Sri Lanka was
famous for its color stones,

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:58.823
and so I made a few visits to
artisanal mines in the area.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:03.710
These are some of of the
most precious commodities

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:08.250
in the world and yet the
people who mine these stones

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.853
live some of the harshest
conditions in the world,

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:13.450
it just didn't make any sense.

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:15.180
And that's when I began my research

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.510
into laboratory grown gemstones,

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:19.790
gemstones cultivated in a science lab

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:21.490
rather than mined from the ground.

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:27.790
So I'm sat here in the
Cotswolds, in our lab,

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:30.440
the place where we created the process

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:32.333
to make these diamonds from the sky.

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:36.130
The most permanent form of carbon

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:38.320
that we know of is a diamond.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:42.210
We set out to do this in
order to lock carbon up

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:43.300
into a permanent form.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:46.263
It's like a carbon capture
and storage project.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.330
But what we learned very quickly
was that there's not a lot

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:52.080
of carbon in a diamond actually,

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.290
maybe four grams in a one carat stone.

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:56.230
But when we looked at the
diamond mining industry

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:58.730
we found that to make
that same one carat stone

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:01.970
requires the digging
of 1,100 tons of rock,

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:05.030
and the exposure of 30 tons of toxic metal

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:06.610
to the environment,

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:09.550
the consumption of 5,000 liters of water,

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:12.963
and the production of half
a ton of greenhouse gas.

00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:16.750
So De Beers have made famous this slogan,

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:18.620
"diamonds are forever".

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:20.410
What they don't tell
you is that the impacts

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:22.890
of diamond mining are forever.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:25.840
So the holes in the big
diamond mines in Russia

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:28.913
for example, are so big,
you can see them from orbit.

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:34.360
And so the avoided carbon
that we are achieving

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:37.110
through making diamonds
from the sky is way bigger

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:39.040
than the carbon that we're
actually pulling from the sky

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:39.913
and locking up.

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:42.870
And what we make are diamonds.

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:46.733
They're chemically, molecularly
identical to diamonds

00:06:46.733 --> 00:06:48.910
that are dug out of the earth.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:55.380
Lab-grown diamonds as a thing
have been around for decades

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:57.460
but they were small,

00:06:57.460 --> 00:06:59.610
and they were used for
industrial diamonds.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:06.620
Gem type diamonds have
begun to be a major industry

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:10.820
in more or less the last 10 years.

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:14.190
The knowledge that they
exist is becoming greater

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:17.950
but people still have confusion

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:19.950
because there have been

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:23.793
for a long, long time, imitation diamonds,

00:07:24.665 --> 00:07:27.930
cubic zirconia, things
that look like diamonds

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:30.160
but are not diamonds.

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:32.790
A lab-grown diamond,
it's not an imitation.

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:36.610
It's not like vegan meat or something,

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:39.933
it is the same thing
made in a different way.

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:47.900
So the magic starts where
we first pull carbon dioxide

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:51.060
from the atmosphere with a
machine from Switzerland.

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:54.223
We then split rain water
into hydrogen and oxygen

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:58.930
and we feed a combination of
hydrogen and carbon dioxide

00:07:58.930 --> 00:07:59.883
to our bugs.

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:04.190
These are ancient bacteria
that take these two ingredients

00:08:04.190 --> 00:08:06.850
and stitch them together into methane,

00:08:06.850 --> 00:08:09.480
and then we feed the methane
along with some other gases

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.483
into our diamond ovens.

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:16.720
So in our diamond oven, we
might lay out 16 diamond seeds

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:17.743
in a square pattern.

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:22.080
And they live of in this ball of plasma

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:24.372
while we feed it with gas.

00:08:24.372 --> 00:08:26.580
It runs at about a 1,000
degrees centigrade,

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:28.190
so it's very hot.

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:31.340
But within this plasma
ball, we can turn our gases

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:33.823
into a crystalline
structure that is a diamond.

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:38.633
And at the end of two weeks,
we pull out a square like this.

00:08:41.170 --> 00:08:44.600
It's like a window with
a, with a black edge.

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:47.740
And we chop this block up
into 16 individual stones

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:50.080
with our laser.

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:51.920
And then we trim each one of those

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:54.300
to remove the black growth
from around the edges

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:57.110
and then we have what we call roughs -

00:08:57.110 --> 00:08:59.150
little squares or cubes of material

00:08:59.150 --> 00:09:00.620
that looks a little translucent.

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:04.010
And we send it away and
it gets cut and polished

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:06.670
into the shape that we all
traditionally associate

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:07.503
with a diamond.

00:09:09.001 --> 00:09:11.243
And is this the, the biggest
one that you've done?

00:09:11.243 --> 00:09:13.326
Yes, the biggest, yeah.

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:19.090
And any given batch will come
out with a different color

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:22.930
or clarity, but we are achieving quality

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:25.180
in the top 1% of all
diamonds, we know this,

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:26.820
we send them away for assessment

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:28.110
in the same way that diamonds

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:31.140
ripped out of the Earth
are sent for assessment.

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:33.933
And we get back the same basic metrics.

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:40.150
Although many sort of laboratory do work

00:09:40.150 --> 00:09:45.150
with renewable energy, the
source of the original elements

00:09:45.580 --> 00:09:49.480
often have to be taken
from existing minerals.

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:53.500
And I think the excitement
with what Skydiamond is doing

00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:57.140
is that it actually takes
something that we don't want,

00:09:57.140 --> 00:09:58.930
something that is of waste,

00:09:58.930 --> 00:10:02.513
and turn that into
something that is treasured.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:07.600
At retail mined diamonds,
traditional diamonds,

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:12.600
cost two or three times more
than lab grown diamonds.

00:10:12.950 --> 00:10:17.950
And I think it's kind of an
old fashioned idea that luxury

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:24.820
requires the most expensive materials.

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:27.830
You can also use more unique designs.

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:32.500
You can use more sort of ingenuity,

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:35.410
many potential sources of value

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:37.473
can make something luxurious.

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:40.730
I think for a younger generation,

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:44.530
the idea that you can have
this scientific breakthrough

00:10:44.530 --> 00:10:49.530
to create this special stone
has a kind of romance to it

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:53.040
just as the old "diamond
is forever" slogan

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:54.640
had a different kind of romance.

00:10:57.870 --> 00:10:59.840
When working with recycled gold,

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:02.370
you can purchase pellets of gold

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:04.930
that actually come from different sources,

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:07.070
whether it's from tech industries,

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:10.340
extracted from discarded electronics

00:11:10.340 --> 00:11:12.680
like computer chips and mobile phones

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:17.580
and also from repurposing
sort of antique jewelry.

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:21.660
So all of this sort of metal
is then remelted together,

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:25.503
and refined into the pellets
that we are using today.

00:11:27.049 --> 00:11:29.220
The process of creating this piece

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:34.220
involves first a wax
model of a blade of grass,

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:37.350
so like carving of a miniature sculpture.

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:39.160
From the wax model,

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:42.980
it is then lost-wax casted into gold -

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:46.650
one of the oldest techniques
within fine jewelry.

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:50.050
So how it works is by
setting that wax piece

00:11:50.050 --> 00:11:51.853
inside a plaster cast,

00:11:53.729 --> 00:11:55.390
and then melting gold pellets,

00:11:55.390 --> 00:11:58.713
and pouring the metal
into the plaster cast.

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:03.400
It will then fill the
void where the wax was.

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:05.110
And so you lose the piece of wax

00:12:05.110 --> 00:12:07.223
hence the term lost-wax casting.

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:10.260
And once the metal has cooled down,

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:12.200
when you open up your cast,

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:13.240
you will get the piece

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:16.260
that resembles exactly your original wax

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:19.883
but in solid gold.

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:28.920
And from that, you will then start working

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:33.383
on finishing the piece
by filing and assembling.

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:35.780
And within the gold piece,

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:39.093
we will then work on the
setting of each gemstone.

00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:50.090
A piece of jewelry finally
sort of come to live

00:12:50.090 --> 00:12:53.123
in those final stages of polishing it,

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:56.540
because that's when you
see the transformation

00:12:56.540 --> 00:12:59.690
of a piece that is maybe
quite dirty and quite gritty,

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:02.713
and that final polish gives it that shine.

00:13:04.872 --> 00:13:09.872
The fine jewelry industry has
always been about prestige.

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:12.220
Whether it's showing off your wealth,

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:14.810
or showing off your power,

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:17.950
what conveys prestige changes over time.

00:13:17.950 --> 00:13:20.770
And if that includes being concerned

00:13:20.770 --> 00:13:23.440
about the materials that things are made,

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:26.720
not just that they'd be expensive or rare,

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:29.190
but that they'd be ethically sourced,

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:31.810
you're going to have to take
that into consideration.

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:34.120
And that opens the way

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:36.993
for lab-grown gems among other things.

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:42.350
To doubters, I would say
come back in 20 years.

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:46.130
Come back in 20 years
and see where we are.

00:13:46.130 --> 00:13:50.740
I suspect that we will
no longer even be talking

00:13:50.740 --> 00:13:52.520
about this distinction.

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:54.223
There will just be diamonds.

00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:02.830
Lady Gaga wore a pair
of our feather earrings

00:14:02.830 --> 00:14:04.850
with laboratory grown Tourmaline.

00:14:04.850 --> 00:14:06.000
And that was, for me,

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:09.023
the first time sort of seeing
it out on the red carpet.

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:14.010
It always blows my mind when a celebrity

00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:15.483
wears one of our pieces.

00:14:17.780 --> 00:14:21.250
I think in the beginning,
people choose it predominantly

00:14:21.250 --> 00:14:24.610
for the design and for its aesthetics.

00:14:24.610 --> 00:14:28.040
But when they find out more
about the sustainability,

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:31.470
and the ethical sort of
ethos behind what we do

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:35.300
and the reasoning and why
we use certain materials,

00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:37.693
they become much more supportive.

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:41.670
The other pair of the broach

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:45.603
features Tahitian pearls
with anodized aluminum.

00:14:48.300 --> 00:14:52.230
So pearls used to be
the most precious gems,

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:57.230
the gems that represented
wealth, that represented power.

00:14:57.910 --> 00:15:00.260
You see a lot of them in Renaissance art

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:03.350
showing how rich the
person wearing them is,

00:15:03.350 --> 00:15:06.670
and the reason the pearls were so valuable

00:15:06.670 --> 00:15:09.310
is that they are extremely rare in nature.

00:15:09.310 --> 00:15:13.200
But in the a late 19th century

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:16.270
someone figured out that
you could culture pearls,

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:20.892
so that you could have a
reliable supply of pearls.

00:15:21.850 --> 00:15:23.050
It became affordable

00:15:23.050 --> 00:15:26.170
because of this
technological breakthrough.

00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:30.880
But if you owned an old string of pearls

00:15:30.880 --> 00:15:34.810
that was worth the price
of a building in Manhattan,

00:15:34.810 --> 00:15:36.483
which is a real example,

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:41.380
suddenly that string of
pearls was worth a lot less.

00:15:41.380 --> 00:15:44.650
And that is the kind of dynamic

00:15:44.650 --> 00:15:47.950
that lab-grown diamonds could present

00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:49.503
for the diamond industry.

00:15:52.650 --> 00:15:54.740
For this particular piece,

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:57.900
I've chosen to use pearls
from Kamoka Pearls.

00:15:57.900 --> 00:16:01.070
I'm always fascinated
by the incredible colors

00:16:01.070 --> 00:16:03.530
they are able to achieve
where you get these sort

00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:07.260
of pistachio hues to
golden, to silvery gray,

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:09.723
to midnight sort of blue.

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:17.263
I came across Kamoka Pearls
around two, three years ago.

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:24.760
What really drew me to them

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:28.550
was the way they farm their
pearls is a perfect example

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:33.460
of how people can work
with nature in harmony

00:16:33.460 --> 00:16:37.193
to actually improve the
environment around them.

00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:46.133
So traditionally, when it
comes to pearl farming,

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:49.770
water jets are used to clean the oysters

00:16:49.770 --> 00:16:51.560
and by using water jets,

00:16:51.560 --> 00:16:55.700
it changes the current
surrounding the oysters,

00:16:55.700 --> 00:16:57.490
it can damage the coral reefs

00:16:57.490 --> 00:17:00.150
and change the pH level of the water,

00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:03.123
and actually disrupts
the whole ecosystems.

00:17:06.074 --> 00:17:11.020
Kamoka Pearls utilizes exotic
fishes to clean their oysters.

00:17:12.530 --> 00:17:14.760
Not only have they been able to regenerate

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:16.823
the reef surrounding their island,

00:17:18.010 --> 00:17:22.430
they've also been able to
regenerate some endangered species

00:17:22.430 --> 00:17:25.753
in the ecosystems, which is phenomenal.

00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:38.700
Making jewelry is a messy process,

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:41.580
and I always say it is like beauty

00:17:41.580 --> 00:17:44.920
emerges from organized chaos.

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:46.540
The process to make this piece

00:17:46.540 --> 00:17:50.670
involves hand shaping aluminum metal,

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:53.923
piercing it out of the
sheet using a piercing saw,

00:17:54.910 --> 00:17:57.710
and it's very much
guided by subtle changes

00:17:57.710 --> 00:18:02.540
of directions to pierce the
piece out into the form,

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:05.913
and then molding it into blade of grass.

00:18:07.890 --> 00:18:12.593
Chasing is setting a
piece of some sheet metal

00:18:12.593 --> 00:18:14.230
that is essentially flat

00:18:14.230 --> 00:18:17.440
into a platform where you shape the metal

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:20.120
by knocking and tapping on the hammer.

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:22.380
So every vein and every grain

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:26.473
has been worked on
individually and organically.

00:18:28.750 --> 00:18:31.160
Aluminum is so much lighter than gold

00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:32.940
or platinum, or silver.

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:35.983
So I'm able to work with
much sort of bolder geometry.

00:18:39.130 --> 00:18:41.840
And at the same time, it is about creating

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:45.130
a new color palette that mimics nature,

00:18:45.130 --> 00:18:47.000
because aluminum can be colored

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:49.260
in a myriad of different ways

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:52.223
compared to traditional precious metals.

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:56.290
You run an electric current into a bath

00:18:56.290 --> 00:18:59.730
where the metal is then submerged into

00:18:59.730 --> 00:19:02.730
sort of different tanks and beakers

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:06.743
that allows the color to be
imbued into the aluminum.

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:11.360
I've chosen a specific type of pearl

00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:15.120
that reflects the colors in the leaves.

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:18.100
And to set the pearl into aluminum,

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:21.900
we then create a cup
with a pin in the center,

00:19:21.900 --> 00:19:23.623
which is known as the setting.

00:19:27.670 --> 00:19:31.150
The pearl is then drilled,
and then to secure in place,

00:19:31.150 --> 00:19:32.453
we use a resin.

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:41.650
I see this pair of broaches

00:19:41.650 --> 00:19:44.300
being equally shared
between sort of partners

00:19:44.300 --> 00:19:45.583
and family members.

00:19:46.730 --> 00:19:50.420
Equally worn by men and
women at the same time.

00:19:50.420 --> 00:19:52.960
They are inspired by the same elements,

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:55.890
presented in a different language,

00:19:55.890 --> 00:19:58.523
yet they complement each
other in the same way.

00:20:06.790 --> 00:20:10.520
I think the future of fine jewelry

00:20:10.520 --> 00:20:14.640
really is about sustainable and ethical

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:17.853
and mindful operations in every sense.

00:20:20.090 --> 00:20:23.490
And it's being able to learn from the past

00:20:23.490 --> 00:20:27.070
to offer different
perspective in the present,

00:20:27.070 --> 00:20:29.503
always in mind for a better future.

00:20:30.552 --> 00:20:35.380
I suppose this is one of my
favorite quotes by Maya Angelou,

00:20:35.380 --> 00:20:38.150
"Do the best you can
until you know better.

00:20:38.150 --> 00:20:40.967
And when you know better, do better."

